Wrap the user-model mutation in a try/catch for ReadOnlyException and fall back to the authentication session:

if (credential == null || RecoveryAuthnCodesUtils.allCodesUsed(credential)) {
    try {
        authenticatedUser.addRequiredAction(UserModel.RequiredAction.CONFIGURE_RECOVERY_AUTHN_CODES);
    } catch (ReadOnlyException e) {
        // Federated read-only user storage cannot persist required actions on the user model.
        // Fall back to the authentication session so the user is prompted to reconfigure
        // recovery codes during this auth flow.
        context.getAuthenticationSession().addRequiredAction(UserModel.RequiredAction.CONFIGURE_RECOVERY_AUTHN_CODES);
    }
}

This mirrors the defensive pattern used in CredentialHelper.createRecoveryCodesCredential() where federated storage is attempted first and local fallback is used when federated storage cannot handle the operation.

Type the deserialization and use symmetric String equality:

```java
List<String> generatedKeys;
try {
    generatedKeys = JsonSerialization.readValue(
        storedRecoveryKeys.getCredentialData(),
        new TypeReference<List<String>>(){});
} catch (IOException e) {
    log.warnf("Cannot deserialize recovery keys credential for the user %s", user.getUsername());
    return false;
}
return generatedKeys.contains(input.getChallengeResponse());
```

This removes the unchecked warning, makes the element type explicit, and guarantees `String.equals` semantics regardless of how Jackson coerces JSON scalars.
